    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d80">

                <pb facs="0228" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg132"/>

                <head>No. 80.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Low to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-02-12"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 12, 1872</date>.
                        (Received April 26.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 132.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: * * * * * * *</p>
                <p>It is proper that I should observe in this connection that the employment of
                    natives as interpreters to our consulates is a serious evil; it is fraught with
                    danger to our interests, and lowers the dignity of the <pb facs="0229" n="133"
                        xml:id="pg133"/> Government in the estimation of the Chinese officials from
                    the highest to the lowest.</p>
                <p>It is only by the greatest scrutiny and constant watchfulness that a consul so
                    situated can be certain that he will not be betrayed.* *</p>
                <p>The insufficient appropriations for consular interpreters involves the necessity
                    of employing such men at the present time; it would, however, be highly
                    conducive to our interests in China were Congress to view this matter in the
                    proper light, and make provision for filling these places with competent
                    persons, who are identified with the honor and interests of our Government and
                    people.</p>
                <p>To me this is so obvious that argument seems entirely unnecessary.</p>
                <p>* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>FREDERICK F. LOW.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
